<p>
    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Sligh can't really interact with the
    combo—no counters, no way to kill an enchantment—so their only path to
    victory is burning you out before you stabilize. When you land an early
    <span class="card-hover" role="button" tabindex="0" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
        >Meddling Mage</a
    >, name <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Lightning Bolt"
        >Lightning Bolt</a
    >. That keeps Mage's 2/2 body around as a blocker against <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Jackal Pup">Jackal Pup</a
    > and their other one-drops. Later in the game, once the board has settled,
    switch to naming their reach—<a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Fireblast">Fireblast</a
    > or <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Ball Lightning">Ball Lightning</a
    >—since that's what actually kills you from a stable board.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> Cut every <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a
    >—paying two life per activation is suicide against burn. Bring in <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
    >, <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Hydroblast">Hydroblast</a
    >, and your fourth <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
    >. After board, don't race them. Treat every removal spell and blocker as
    a trade that eats one of their burn cards and buys you time—the goal is to
    reach your combo alive, not to win a damage race.<br />
    <em>Which hate cards actually pull their weight:</em>
    <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Chill">Chill</span> is the MVP.
    Once you've stabilized at a low life total, it shuts off their last outs:
    topdecked Mountains into cheap burn.
    <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Sphere of Law"
        >Sphere of Law</a
    > gives you huge protection, but it dies to a splashed naturalize effect and
    is too slow on the draw. Leave <strong>Circle of Protection: Red</strong>
    at home; taxing your own mana every turn just gets in the way of setting up
    and comboing off.
</p>
